    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2026 EICMA's Speed Legends Exhibit Was Not To Be Missed – See the Bikes That Paved MotoGP History\nCourt Orders JLR To Destroy Seized BYD Files in 24 Hours\n8 photos\nPhoto: BYD\nA Dutch court has ordered Jaguar Land Rover to destroy the digital files seized from BYD’s Dutch offices or face a penalty of 50,000 euros per hour. JLR chose the Dutch court because it was counting on the Netherlands’ strict intellectual property laws.\n50,000 euros or roughly $55,460. That is how much Jaguar Land Rover has to pay for every single hour of delay until it destroys the digital files seized from BYD. Judge H.F.R. van Heemstra issued the preliminary ruling at the District Court of The Hague, overturning an evidence raid conducted over intellectual property claims involving the BYD Ti7 SUV in August.\nThe ruling immediately invalidates JLR's earlier seizure and sets strict compliance deadlines. This means that JLR must instruct the court-appointed custodian and bailiff to destroy all seized files within 24 hours. The records had been held by an independent custodian and were never handed directly to JLR.\nFailing to issue the destruction order triggers a fine of 50,000 euros per hour, capped at 500,000 euros (approximately $555,000). JLR must also provide BYD with independent confirmation within 24 hours after the data is entirely deleted.\nPhoto: tthg 04 | TikTok The court banned JLR from taking further steps under the original seizure order, attaching a separate 500,000-euro penalty for each violation. Furthermore, the judge dismissed JLR’s request to fine BYD 500,000 euros for alleged non-compliance with the original raid, finding provisionally that BYD had cooperated. However, BYD was still ordered to pay 25,376.57 euros ($28,110) in legal costs, the Chinese media reports .\nJaguar Land Rover has been there before. Back in 2020, it lost a trial in London, when it tried to trademark the 3D shape of the classic Land Rover Defender 90 and 110 in an attempt to block entrepreneur Sir Jim Ratclie from building the Ineos Grenadier .\nJLR appealed the decision to the High Court of England and Wales, where Judge Melissa Clarke dismissed the appeal in August 2020. The Grenadier went into production unbothered in July 2022, and deliveries started later that year, in December.\nThis time, it all started back in August 2026 when bailiffs acting on behalf of JLR raided BYD's European offices , which are located in Schiedam and Hoofddorp, the Netherlands, secure internal digital records in a surprise move.\nPhoto: tthg 04 | TikTok BYD labeled the seizure as an overly broad fishing expedition that swept up unrelated proprietary research and development data. JLR reportedly asked for the raid to be fast-tracked so that the suspicious files would be secured before they could potentially be altered or deleted.\nJLR accused BYD of dismantling a Land Rover Defender to reverse-engineer its technology and copying its boxy exterior design for the Ti7 SUV , which is sold in China as the Fangcheng Bao Ti7. The model has yet to launch in Europe, with UK deliveries slated for early 2027. The Ti7 undercuts Land Rover Defender in its home market by nearly 20,000 pounds ($26,740).\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nJaguar Land Rover JLR Land Rover Defender BYD BYD Ti7\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT The 2027 Hopper 5 WL Is a Little Tin Box That Lacks for Nothing",
